Let's outline the key steps: \[
\int\frac{dt}{1-t^{2}+t^{4}}=
\frac{1}{4\sqrt 3}\,
\ln\!\frac{t^{2}+\sqrt 3\,t+1}{\,t^{2}-\sqrt 3\,t+1}
\;+\;\frac12\,
\arctan\!\left(\frac{t}{1-t^{2}}\right)+C .
\]

**Derivation**

\[
1-t^{2}+t^{4}=t^{4}-t^{2}+1
          =(t^{2}+\sqrt3\,t+1)(t^{2}-\sqrt3\,t+1)
\]

Hence

\[
\frac{1}{t^{4}-t^{2}+1}
 =\frac{At+B}{t^{2}+\sqrt3\,t+1}
   +\frac{Ct+D}{t^{2}-\sqrt3\,t+1},
\]

and solving for the constants gives  

\[
A=\frac{1}{2\sqrt3},\;B=\frac12,\qquad
C=-\frac{1}{2\sqrt3},\;D=\frac12 .
\]

Thus

\[
\frac{1}{t^{4}-t^{2}+1}
 =\frac{\frac{t}{2\sqrt3}+\frac12}{t^{2}+\sqrt3 t+1}
   +\frac{-\frac{t}{2\sqrt3}+\frac12}{t^{2}-\sqrt3 t+1}.
\]

Write each numerator as a linear combination of the derivative of the
corresponding denominator plus a constant:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\frac{t}{2\sqrt3}+\frac12}{t^{2}+\sqrt3 t+1}
 &=\frac{1}{4\sqrt3}\,\frac{2t+\sqrt3}{t^{2}+\sqrt3 t+1}
   +\frac14\frac{1}{t^{2}+\sqrt3 t+1},\\[2mm]
\frac{-\frac{t}{2\sqrt3}+\frac12}{t^{2}-\sqrt3 t+1}
 &= -\frac{1}{4\sqrt3}\,\frac{2t-\sqrt3}{t^{2}-\sqrt3 t+1}
   +\frac14\frac{1}{t^{2}-\sqrt3 t+1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Integrating term‑by‑term,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\int\frac{2t+\sqrt3}{t^{2}+\sqrt3 t+1}\,dt &=\ln\!\bigl(t^{2}+\sqrt3 t+1\bigr),\\
\int\frac{2t-\sqrt3}{t^{2}-\sqrt3 t+1}\,dt &=\ln\!\bigl(t^{2}-\sqrt3 t+1\bigr),\\
\int\frac{dt}{t^{2}\pm\sqrt3 t+1}
   &=\int\frac{dt}{\bigl(t\pm\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\bigr)^{2}+\bigl(\frac12\bigr)^{2}}
     =2\arctan\!\bigl(2t\pm\sqrt3\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

Collecting the pieces,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\int\frac{dt}{1-t^{2}+t^{4}}
&=\frac{1}{4\sqrt3}\ln\frac{t^{2}+\sqrt3 t+1}{t^{2}-\sqrt3 t+1}
  +\frac12\Bigl[\arctan(2t+\sqrt3)+\arctan(2t-\sqrt3)\Bigr]+C.
\end{aligned}
\]

Using the addition formula for arctangent,

\[
\arctan(2t+\sqrt3)+\arctan(2t-\sqrt3)
   =\arctan\!\frac{(2t+\sqrt3)+(2t-\sqrt3)}{1-(2t+\sqrt3)(2t-\sqrt3)}
   =\arctan\!\frac{t}{1-t^{2}},
\]

so the antiderivative can be written more compactly as

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle
\int\frac{dt}{1-t^{2}+t^{4}}
= \frac{1}{4\sqrt3}\ln\!\frac{t^{2}+\sqrt3 t+1}{t^{2}-\sqrt3 t+1}
  +\frac12\arctan\!\left(\frac{t}{1-t^{2}}\right)+C }.
\]

We can break it down as follows: **Solution**

\[
\frac{\displaystyle 2a^{2}(b+c)^{2n}-\frac12}{\displaystyle an^{2}-a^{3}-2a^{2}-a}\;:\;
\frac{\displaystyle 2a(b+c)^{n}-1}{\displaystyle a^{2}c-a(nc-c)}
\]

means  

\[
\frac{2a^{2}(b+c)^{2n}-\frac12}{an^{2}-a^{3}-2a^{2}-a}\;
\Bigg/\;
\frac{2a(b+c)^{n}-1}{a^{2}c-a(nc-c)}
=
\frac{2a^{2}(b+c)^{2n}-\frac12}{an^{2}-a^{3}-2a^{2}-a}\;
\cdot\;
\frac{a^{2}c-a(nc-c)}{2a(b+c)^{n}-1}.
\]

---

### 1.  Factor the denominators  

\[
\begin{aligned}
an^{2}-a^{3}-2a^{2}-a
&=a\bigl(n^{2}-a^{2}-2a-1\bigr)\\
&=a\bigl[n^{2}-(a+1)^{2}\bigr]\\
&=a\,(n-a-1)(n+a+1),
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
a^{2}c-a(nc-c)
&=a^{2}c-a\bigl(c(n-1)\bigr)\\
&=ac\bigl(a-(n-1)\bigr)\\
&=ac\,(a-n+1).
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 2.  Factor the numerators  

\[
\begin{aligned}
2a^{2}(b+c)^{2n}-\frac12
&=\frac12\Bigl(4a^{2}(b+c)^{2n}-1\Bigr)\\[2mm]
&=\frac12\Bigl[\,\bigl(2a(b+c)^{n}\bigr)^{2}-1\Bigr]\\[2mm]
&=\frac12\bigl(2a(b+c)^{n}-1\bigr)\bigl(2a(b+c)^{n}+1\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

The second numerator is already simple: \(2a(b+c)^{n}-1\).

---

### 3.  Put everything together  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Expression}
&=\frac{\displaystyle\frac12\bigl(2a(b+c)^{n}-1\bigr)\bigl(2a(b+c)^{n}+1\bigr)}
      {\displaystyle a\,(n-a-1)(n+a+1)}
   \cdot
   \frac{\displaystyle ac\,(a-n+1)}
        {\displaystyle 2a(b+c)^{n}-1}\\[4mm]
&=\frac12\,
   \frac{\bigl(2a(b+c)^{n}+1\bigr)\;ac\;(a-n+1)}
        {a\,(n-a-1)(n+a+1)}\qquad
   \bigl(\text{cancel }2a(b+c)^{n}-1\bigr)\\[4mm]
&=\frac12\,
   \frac{c\;(2a(b+c)^{n}+1)\;(a-n+1)}
        {(n-a-1)(n+a+1)}\qquad
   \bigl(\text{cancel }a\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

Notice that  

\[
a-n+1=-(n-a-1),
\]

so the factor \((n-a-1)\) cancels, leaving a minus sign:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Expression}
&=-\frac12\,
   \frac{c\;(2a(b+c)^{n}+1)}
        {n+a+1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

## Final simplified form  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle
-\frac{c\bigl(2a(b+c)^{n}+1\bigr)}{2\,(n+a+1)}}
\]

(Valid for all values of the symbols for which the original denominators are non‑zero.)

A concise solution goes like this: The quotient  

\[
\left(\frac{f}{g}\right)(x)=\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
      =\frac{\dfrac{11}{x-10}}{\dfrac{13}{x+11}}
      =\frac{11}{x-10}\cdot\frac{x+11}{13}
      =\frac{11(x+11)}{13(x-10)} .
\]

For this expression to be defined we need:

1. **\(f(x)\) defined:** \(x-10\neq 0\;\Rightarrow\;x\neq 10\).
2. **\(g(x)\) defined:** \(x+11\neq 0\;\Rightarrow\;x\neq -11\).
3. **\(g(x)\neq 0\):** Since \(g(x)=\dfrac{13}{x+11}\) has a non‑zero numerator, it is never zero wherever it is defined. Thus no extra restriction beyond the one already listed.

Hence the only points excluded from the real line are \(x=10\) and \(x=-11\).

\[
\boxed{\text{Domain } = \mathbb{R}\setminus\{10,\,-11\}
      =(-\infty,-11)\cup(-11,10)\cup(10,\infty).}
\]

