Here's the core idea and its execution: The game has  

\[
9\text{ rooms}+6\text{ people}+6\text{ weapons}=21\text{ cards}
\]

and the solution consists of one card of each type.  
Before any cards are dealt there are  

\[
9\cdot 6\cdot 6 = 324
\]

equally‑likely solutions.

The first player receives 4 cards.  
He knows that none of his own cards can be in the solution, so his
guess must be a **room, a person and a weapon that are not in his hand**.
If his hand contains  

* \(r\) rooms,
* \(p\) people,
* \(w\) weapons   \(\;(r+p+w=4)\),

then the numbers of still‑possible cards are  

\[
9-r\text{ rooms},\qquad 6-p\text{ people},\qquad 6-w\text{ weapons}.
\]

Given such a hand, the solution is uniformly distributed among the
\((9-r)(6-p)(6-w)\) remaining triples, so the probability that a random
guess (chosen uniformly from those triples) is correct is  

\[
\frac{1}{(9-r)(6-p)(6-w)} .
\]

We must average this over all possible hands of four cards.  
The number of ways to receive a hand with composition \((r,p,w)\) is  

\[
\binom{9}{r}\binom{6}{p}\binom{6}{w},
\]

and the total number of 4‑card hands is \(\displaystyle\binom{21}{4}=5985\).

Hence  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\Pr(\text{first guess correct}) &=
\sum_{r+p+w=4}
\frac{\displaystyle\binom{9}{r}\binom{6}{p}\binom{6}{w}}
     {\displaystyle\binom{21}{4}}\;
\frac{1}{(9-r)(6-p)(6-w)} \\[4pt]
&= \frac{1}{5985}\sum_{r+p+w=4}
   \frac{\binom{9}{r}\binom{6}{p}\binom{6}{w}}
        {(9-r)(6-p)(6-w)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Evaluating the sum over the fifteen possible triples \((r,p,w)\) gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\Pr(\text{correct}) &=
\frac{126}{5985\cdot180}
+\frac{504}{5985\cdot180}
+\frac{504}{5985\cdot180}
+\frac{540}{5985\cdot168}
+\frac{1296}{5985\cdot175}
+\frac{540}{5985\cdot168}\\
&\qquad
+\frac{180}{5985\cdot144}
+\frac{810}{5985\cdot160}
+\frac{810}{5985\cdot160}
+\frac{180}{5985\cdot144}
+\frac{15}{5985\cdot108}
+\frac{120}{5985\cdot135}\\
&\qquad
+\frac{225}{5985\cdot144}
+\frac{120}{5985\cdot135}
+\frac{15}{5985\cdot108}\\[4pt]
&\approx 0.00608 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the probability that the first player’s very first guess (made
with only his four cards) is exactly the solution is  

\[
\boxed{\;0.0061\text{ (approximately)}\;}
\]

or about **0.61 %**, i.e. roughly **1 chance in 164**.

First the conclusion, then the reasoning: The selections are independent, and each day a member is chosen from the 32‑person team.

\[
\begin{aligned}
P(\text{senior}) &=\frac{7}{32},\\
P(\text{junior}) &=\frac{9}{32},\\
P(\text{sophomore}) &=\frac{10}{32},\\
P(\text{freshman}) &=\frac{6}{32}.
\end{aligned}
\]

For 6 days we need exactly 2 seniors, 2 juniors, 1 sophomore and 1 freshman.  
Using the multinomial formula,

\[
\begin{aligned}
P &=\frac{6!}{2!\,2!\,1!\,1!}
\left(\frac{7}{32}\right)^{2}
\left(\frac{9}{32}\right)^{2}
\left(\frac{10}{32}\right)^{1}
\left(\frac{6}{32}\right)^{1} \\[4pt]
&=180\;\frac{7^{2}\,9^{2}\,10\,6}{32^{6}} \\[4pt]
&= \frac{42\,865\,200}{1\,073\,741\,824}\\[4pt]
&\approx 0.0399213.
\end{aligned}
\]

Rounded to four significant digits, the probability is  

\[
\boxed{0.03992}
\]

