A concise solution goes like this: \[
\frac{\displaystyle \sqrt{ab}-\frac{ab}{a+\sqrt{ab}}}
      {\displaystyle\frac{\sqrt[4]{ab}-\sqrt b}{a-b}}
\]

---

### 1.  Introduce square‑root variables  

Let  

\[
x=\sqrt a,\qquad y=\sqrt b\qquad (x>0,\;y>0)
\]

so that  

\[
a=x^{2},\; b=y^{2},\;
\sqrt{ab}=xy,\;
\sqrt[4]{ab}=\sqrt{xy},\;
\sqrt b = y .
\]

---

### 2.  Simplify the numerator  

\[
\begin{aligned}
N &= xy-\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{x^{2}+xy}
   = xy\Bigl(1-\frac{xy}{x^{2}+xy}\Bigr)\\[2mm]
  &= xy\Bigl(1-\frac{y}{x+y}\Bigr)
   = xy\frac{x}{x+y}
   =\frac{x^{2}y}{x+y}.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 3.  Simplify the denominator  

\[
\begin{aligned}
D &=\frac{\sqrt{xy}-y}{x^{2}-y^{2}}
   =\frac{\sqrt{y}\,(\sqrt{x}-\sqrt{y})}
          {(x-y)(x+y)}\\[2mm]
  &=\frac{\sqrt{y}\,(\sqrt{x}-\sqrt{y})}
          {(\sqrt{x}-\sqrt{y})(\sqrt{x}+\sqrt{y})(x+y)}\\[2mm]
  &=\frac{\sqrt{y}}{(\sqrt{x}+\sqrt{y})(x+y)}.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 4.  Form the whole fraction  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{N}{D}
&=\frac{\displaystyle\frac{x^{2}y}{x+y}}
        {\displaystyle\frac{\sqrt{y}}{(\sqrt{x}+\sqrt{y})(x+y)}}\\[4mm]
&= \frac{x^{2}y}{x+y}\cdot
   \frac{(\sqrt{x}+\sqrt{y})(x+y)}{\sqrt{y}}\\[2mm]
&=x^{2}y\;\frac{\sqrt{x}+\sqrt{y}}{\sqrt{y}}\\[2mm]
&=x^{2}\sqrt{y}\,(\sqrt{x}+\sqrt{y})\\[2mm]
&=x^{\frac52}y^{\frac12}+x^{2}y.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 5.  Return to \(a,b\)

Recall \(x=\sqrt a = a^{1/2}\) and \(y=\sqrt b = b^{1/2}\).

\[
\begin{aligned}
x^{\frac52}y^{\frac12}
   &= a^{5/4}b^{1/4}=a\,(ab)^{1/4},\\[2mm]
x^{2}y &= a\,b^{1/2}=a\sqrt b .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence  

\[
\frac{N}{D}=a\bigl((ab)^{1/4}+\sqrt b\bigr)
          =a\bigl(\sqrt[4]{ab}+\sqrt b\bigr).
\]

---

\[
\boxed{\,a\bigl(\sqrt[4]{ab}+\sqrt b\bigr)\,}
\]

Here's a clear solution: The intersection \((x(c),y(c))\) of the two lines is obtained by solving  

\[
\begin{cases}
3x+5y=1\\[2pt]
(2+c)x+5c^{2}y=1
\end{cases}
\qquad (c\neq 1)
\]

---

### 1. Solve the linear system

Write the system in matrix form  

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
3 & 5\\[2pt]
2+c & 5c^{2}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}.
\]

The determinant is  

\[
\Delta = 3\cdot5c^{2}-5(2+c)=15c^{2}-5c-10
      =5\bigl(3c^{2}-c-2\bigr)
      =5(3c+2)(c-1).
\]

Using Cramer’s rule,

\[
\begin{aligned}
x &=\frac{1\cdot5c^{2}-5\cdot1}{\Delta}
   =\frac{5(c^{2}-1)}{\Delta}
   =\frac{c^{2}-1}{(3c+2)(c-1)}
   =\frac{(c-1)(c+1)}{(3c+2)(c-1)}
   =\frac{c+1}{3c+2},\\[6pt]
y &=\frac{3\cdot1-(2+c)\cdot1}{\Delta}
   =\frac{3-(2+c)}{\Delta}
   =\frac{1-c}{\Delta}
   =-\frac{c-1}{5(3c+2)(c-1)}
   =-\frac{1}{5(3c+2)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus for every \(c\neq1\)

\[
\boxed{\,\displaystyle
(x(c),y(c))=\left(\frac{c+1}{3c+2},\;-\frac{1}{5(3c+2)}\right)}.
\]

---

### 2. Take the limit as \(c\to 1\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{c\to1}x(c) &= \frac{1+1}{3\cdot1+2}= \frac{2}{5},\\[4pt]
\lim_{c\to1}y(c) &= -\frac{1}{5(3\cdot1+2)}= -\frac{1}{5\cdot5}= -\frac{1}{25}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence the intersection point approaches  

\[
\boxed{\left(\frac{2}{5},\;-\frac{1}{25}\right)}.
\]

---

**Remark.** When \(c=1\) the two equations become identical \((3x+5y=1)\), so there are infinitely many intersection points. The limit above gives the unique point that the intersection approaches as the second line “coincides” with the first one.

